BRIEF COMMUNICATIONS


On F o u r - S h e e t e d P o l y n o m i a l Mappings of C2. T h e General Case

   A. V. Domrina



      KgY WORDS: polynomial mapping of the complex plane, topological degree, Jacobian hypothesis, dicrltical
      component, splice diagram.




                                                        Introduction
   Let ~-2 and C a be two copies of the complex plane, f : ~a --r C 2 be a polynomial mapping whose
Jacobian is a nonzero constant. The well-known Jacobian hypothesis asserts that such a mapping f is
polynomlally invertible (see surveys in [1, 2]). Our mapping f is polynomiai/y invertible if a n d only if a
generic point in C 2 has exactly one preimage. The number of preimages of a generic point is called the
topological degree of the mapping. It is known that polynomial mappings whose Jacobian is a nonzero
constant cannot have topological degree 2 or 3(see [3]). In this paper we consider the ease of degree 4.
The main result is the following theorem

  T h e o r e m . If a polynomial mapping f : ~a __~ C 2 has topological degree 4, then its Jacobian is not a
nonzero constant.
   This paper continues the research started in the paper [4], in which the theorem is proved for the case
in which the mapping f has one dicritical component. Here we discuss the remaining cases.

