./xmodel/sol-pc.log:1325:xmodel/grok-census11a-review.md:106:  **Family termination.** The last six rows are `w=2/(2k+1)`, μ₀=15..25 odd, κ̄=6, one exact-fit route (`λ_pre=5`, `bleft=0`, `ψ=1`, tot = budget = 5). Next member would be μ₀=27, `w=2/27`, `ν_G=79`, κ̄=6. The closure's minimum weight is `2/25`; `w≤2/27` is empty; `w=2/27` is absent. Stronger than claimed: inversion against *every* priced weight produces **zero** hits at μ₀≥27 (so no *other* cell sneaks in at large μ₀ on a different `w`). The inversion loops `c=1..c(g₀)` per (I5d) — finite, no `ν`-cap, no `μ₀`-cap — and the engine iterates all 69 states. `px5.NCAP=400` is not on this path. Closure-forced.
./xmodel/sol-pc.log:1392:xmodel/grok-mo-review.md:54:  - Nobody machine-checked Orevkov 1987, Domrina–Orevkov 1998, or Żołądek 2008. Those are different formalisms (splice diagrams; Newton–Puiseux charts). A book with zero rows in a repaired Sigray frame is consistent with those theorems; it does not recover them.
./xmodel/sol-pc.log:1549:xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:3046:SHEET6.md:10:- **1c. RECON correction**: "no independent reproof of ≤5 exists" is WRONG. Sigray's thesis Theorem 9.1 proves td(f,g) ≥ 6 for any counterexample, independently of Żołądek, same year, different machinery (decorated Eggers–Wall trees vs Newton–Puiseux charts). Never journal-published (zbMATH lists only his monodromy + kp′q−lpq′ papers) but cited as the bound by Makar-Limanov (arXiv:2106.06869) and Borisov (arXiv:1901.04073: "current best lower bound ... is 6"). Caveats on BOTH proofs: Żołądek's neighboring gcd sections have a **documented gap** (GGV arXiv:1401.1784: Lemma 4.10 "claims without proof that I₂ ⊂ (1/m)Γ(f₂)"; his claimed B>33 fails, B≥16 stands only via GGV's independent reproof) — his topdeg-5 sections have no reported gap but are unaudited; Sigray's thesis is unrefereed. Also flagged: arXiv:2407.13795 (Moskowicz, 2024, 8pp) claims NO Keller map has prime field-extension degree — unpublished, treat as unvetted; if ever verified it would exclude 7 and make 6, 8, 9 the whole frontier.
./xmodel/as109-degree-cross-review-grok-20260824.md:136:This `d` is the generic mapping degree of the dominant morphism `A^2_K -> A^2_K`: it is the separable degree of the function-field extension, equivalently the number of geometric points of a generic fibre after extending scalars to an algebraic closure of `M`. Campaign language (`td`, sheet number, Żołądek topological degree) uses this invariant, not the Brouwer degree of large real 4-spheres. For a nonproper étale map the latter need not stabilize; the gate never feeds a sphere-degree to the sheet ledger. Precision, not a gap: wherever this review writes “topological degree”, it means the algebraic mapping degree.
./xmodel/schenk-audit-b-sol56-20260901.md:195:The charged integration states only that, under H2 (irreducible \(A_F\)), an affine-image dicritical with \(\mu=1\) is excluded; it explicitly leaves reducible \(A_F\) configurations at \(N=5\) and above (integration lines 22–35 and 65–73). Ramified dicriticals with \(\mu\ge2\) remain compatible with the promoted local identities and weighted budgets. Schenk claims much more: U-HIT would remove every horizontal source-boundary curve, without H2, degree restrictions, branch-shape hypotheses, or budget analysis.
