33-1. Introduction
--
44-     The algebraic approach was developed mainly by Abhyankar [1]. It relies on expansions of the component polynomials
45-f , g and of the Jacobian Jac(f , g) with respect to quasi-homogeneous gradations defined by means of edges of the Newton
46-diagrams of the polynomials f , g. One obtains similarity of the Newton diagrams, reduction of their sizes and obstructions
47-to the highest quasi-homogeneous parts of f and g. Further development in this direction was continued in the works of
48-Moh [19], Appelgate and Onishi [2], Heitmann [14], Oka [25], Nagata [20,21], Nowicki and Nakai [24] and others. In particular,
49:the Jacobian Conjecture was proved for maps with deg P ≤ 101, with gcd(deg f , deg g) being a prime number and with
50-gcd(deg f , deg g) < 16.
51-     The topological approach was initiated by Vitushkin [31,32]. It essentially relies on resolution of indeterminacies of f and
52-g at infinity. One obtains a holomorphic map between compact algebraic surfaces, which should be ramified (for a counter-
53-example to the Jacobian Conjecture). In [31] an example of such a map is constructed in the topological category; it is 3–fold
54-and is ramified along some divisor at ‘infinity’. The paper [31] is very technical, but later Orevkov [29] explained Vitushkin’s
